VI. Ishtar og Himmeltyren
VI. Ishtar and the Bull of Heaven

Gilgamesh vaskede kampens spor af sig og rensede sit sønderrevne tøj. Han flettede håret, så det hang ned over skuldrene, lagde det snavsede tøj væk og tog rent på. Han tog armringe på og spændte sit sværdbælte. Gilgamesh bandt båndet om panden og spændte sit sværdbælte.
Gilgamesh washed away the marks of battle and cleaned his torn clothes. He braided his hair to hang over his shoulders, put aside his soiled clothes and dressed in clean ones. He put on armlets and buckled his sword belt. Gilgamesh tied his headband and buckled his sword belt.

Gudinden Ishtar så, hvor smuk Gilgamesh var. »Kom, Gilgamesh, gift dig med mig. Giv mig rigeligt af din krops frugt. Du skal være min mand, og jeg skal være din hustru. Jeg giver dig en vogn af lapisblåt og guld, hjulet af guld, åget af ædelsten. Hver dag skal store muldyr spændes for den. Kom ind i vores hus, der dufter af ceder. Når du træder ind, skal tærsklen og forhøjningen kysse dine fødder. Konger, fyrster og herskere skal bøje sig for dig og bringe dig bjergenes og sletternes afgrøder som skat. Dine geder skal få mange kid, dine får tvillingelam, og dine æsler skal vokse sig så store som muldyr. Dine vognheste skal blive berømte for deres løb. Ingen muldyr under åg skal måle sig med dine.«
The goddess Ishtar saw how beautiful Gilgamesh was. “Come, Gilgamesh, marry me. Give me freely of the fruit of your body. You shall be my husband and I your wife. I'll equip you with a chariot of lapis blue and gold, its wheel of gold, its yoke of precious stones. Great mules will be harnessed to it every day. Come into our cedar-scented house. As you enter, the threshold and the dais will kiss your feet. Kings, princes and rulers will bow to you and bring the produce of mountain and plain in tribute. Your goats will have many kids, your ewes twin lambs, and your donkeys will grow as large as mules. Your chariot horses will win fame for their speed. No mules in harness will equal yours.”

Gilgamesh svarede Ishtar: »Men hvad skal jeg give dig, hvis jeg gifter mig med dig? Jeg skal skaffe dig olie til kroppen og klæder at tage på. Jeg skal give dig brød og mad i mængder, der passer sig for en gudinde, og drikke som til en dronning. Jeg vil være bundet […] lad os samle […] klæde i en dragt. Hvad vinder jeg ved at gifte mig med dig?
Gilgamesh answered Ishtar, “But what must I give you if I marry you? I must supply oil for your body and clothes to wear. I must give you bread and food in quantities fit for a goddess, and drink fit for a queen. I will be bound […] let us amass […] clothe in a garment. What do I gain by marrying you?

Du er en ruin, hvor ingen kan søge ly for vejret. En bagdør, der giver efter for vind og storm. Et palads, der knuser krigerne indenfor. En faldgrube, hvor dækket giver efter. Beg, der sviner den til, som bærer den. En dunk, der lækker ned ad den, som bærer den. Kalksten, der får stenmuren til at smuldre. En amulet af kalcedon, der svigter i fjendeland. En sandal, der får sin ejer til at snuble på vejen.
“You are a ruin that gives no one shelter from the weather. A back door that gives way to wind and storm. A palace that crushes the warriors inside. A pit whose covering gives way. Pitch that soils whoever carries it. A flask that leaks over the one who bears it. Limestone that makes a stone wall crumble. A chalcedony charm that fails its wearer in enemy country. A sandal that trips its owner on the road.

Hvilken af dine mænd elskede du trofast til det sidste? Hvilken af dine herrer fik overtaget over dig? Lad mig rulle den lange række af mænd op for dig. Du kan selv sige, om det passer. Tammuz, din ungdoms mand, får du hvert år folk til at græde over. Så elskede du den farvestrålende ellekrage. Du slog den og brækkede dens vinge. Nu står den i lunden og skriger: Kappi! Min vinge! Du elskede en løve, mens den stod i sin fulde kraft, og gravede syv og atter syv fangstgrave til den.
“Which husband did you love faithfully to the end? Which of your lords ever gained the upper hand over you? Let me unfold the long succession of your husbands. You can say yourself whether it's true. Tammuz, the husband of your youth, you make people weep for every year. Then you loved the bright-feathered roller. You struck it and broke its wing. Now it stands in the grove crying: Kappi! My wing! You loved a lion in its full strength and dug seven and seven more pits to catch it.

Du elskede hingsten, prægtig i kamp. Du gav den bidsel, spore og pisk. Du lod den galopere halvtreds mil, til den segnede af sved og træthed. Du gav dens mor Silili grund til at klage. Du elskede hyrden, kvægdriveren, som dag efter dag dyngede trækul op og slagtede gedekid til dig. Du slog ham og gjorde ham til en sjakal. Hans egen hyrdedreng jagede ham bort, og hans hunde bed ham i bagdelen.
“You loved the stallion, magnificent in battle. You gave him bridle, spur and whip. You made him gallop fifty miles to sweat and exhaustion. You gave his mother Silili cause to mourn. You loved the shepherd, the cattle herder who day after day heaped up charcoal and slaughtered young goats for you. You struck him and turned him into a jackal. His own herdboy drove him away and his dogs tore at his buttocks.

Du elskede også Ishullanu, din fars gartner. Hver dag kom han med lækkerier og pyntede dit fad. Du behøvede kun at se ham, så var du forelsket: ‘Kom, min Ishullanu, lad mig smage din kraft. Ræk hånden frem og […]’. [Udgaven fra 1928 udelader to ord her.] Ishullanu svarede: ‘Hvad beder du mig om? Jeg har kun spist det, min mor har bagt. Det, du vil give mig at spise, er syndens og urettens brød. Og sivene giver mig ly for vinteren.’ Da du hørte hans svar, slog du ham og gjorde ham til en edderkop [?]. Du lod ham bo midt oppe i et hus [?]. Han kunne ikke kravle op uden at blive skyllet ned eller kravle ned uden at blive knust. Nu ville du elske mig og siden behandle mig som dem.«
“You also loved Ishullanu, your father's gardener. Every day he brought you delicacies and dressed your platter. One look at him and you were in love: ‘Come, my Ishullanu, let me taste your strength. Reach out your hand and […].’ [The 1928 edition omits two words here.] Ishullanu answered, ‘What are you asking of me? I've eaten only what my mother baked. What you would have me eat is the bread of wrongdoing and sin. And the reeds give me shelter against winter.’ When you heard his answer you struck him and turned him into a spider [?]. You made him live halfway up a house [?]. He couldn't go up without being washed down or go down without being crushed. Now you would love me and then treat me as you treated them.”

Ishtar hørte det, og i raseri steg hun op til himlen. Hun opsøgte sin far Anu og sin mor Antu. »Far, Gilgamesh overøser mig med fornærmelser! Gilgamesh remser mine synder op, alt det onde jeg har gjort.« Anu svarede: »Du bad ham jo give dig rigeligt af sin krops frugt. Derfor remser han dine synder op, alt det onde du har gjort.«
Ishtar heard him and went up to heaven in a rage. She sought out her father Anu and her mother Antu. “Father, Gilgamesh heaps insults on me! Gilgamesh recites my sins, all the wrongs I have done.” Anu answered, “You asked him to give you freely of the fruit of his body. That's why he recites your sins, all the wrongs you have done.”

Ishtar sagde til sin far Anu: »Far, skab mig en himmeltyr, der kan overvinde Gilgamesh. Fyld dens krop med ild […]. Hvis du ikke skaber tyren, så […] Jeg slår […] jeg sætter […] jeg […] Flere end […] vil […] være.« Anu svarede: »Hvis jeg skaber den himmeltyr, du beder om, kommer der syv år, hvor kornaksene står tomme. Vil du samle korn til menneskene og skaffe mere foder til kvæget?« Ishtar svarede sin far Anu: »Jeg har lagt korn til side til menneskene og dyrket foder til kvæget. Hvis der kommer syv år med tomme aks efter tyrens angreb, vil jeg samle korn til menneskene og skaffe mere foder til kvæget.«
Ishtar said to her father Anu, “Father, make me a Bull of Heaven to overcome Gilgamesh. Fill its body with fire […]. If you won't make the bull, then […] I will strike […] I will set […] I will […] More than […] will be […].” Anu answered, “If I make the Bull of Heaven you ask for, seven years of empty husks will follow. Will you gather grain for the people and increase the cattle's fodder?” Ishtar answered her father Anu, “I've stored grain for the people and grown fodder for the cattle. If seven years of empty husks follow the bull's attack, I'll gather grain for the people and increase the cattle's fodder.”

[Omkring syv linjer er stærkt beskadiget. Kampen står i Uruk. Hundrede mænd går mod tyren. Dens ånde tilintetgør dem. To hundrede følger efter, så tre hundrede. Også dem overvinder den.] Enkidu spændte bæltet [?], sprang frem og greb Himmeltyren ved hornene. Han kastede den i sin fulde længde til jorden foran sig […] ved haleroden. [Tretten beskadigede linjer.] Enkidu satte efter den […] Himmeltyren […] greb fat […] ved haleroden […]. [Omkring fjorten beskadigede linjer. Tyren bliver dræbt.]
[About seven lines are badly damaged. The fight is in Uruk. A hundred men move against the bull. Its breath destroys them. Two hundred follow, then three hundred. It overcomes them too.] Enkidu tightened his belt [?], leapt forward and seized the Bull of Heaven by the horns. He threw it down full length before him […] by the root of its tail. [Thirteen damaged lines.] Enkidu chased it […] the Bull of Heaven […] took hold […] by the root of its tail […]. [About fourteen damaged lines. The bull is killed.]

Da de havde dræbt Himmeltyren, tog de hjertet ud og ofrede det til solguden. Da de havde hældt drikofferet ud til solen, satte de to brødre sig ned. Ishtar steg op på Uruks høje mur og gik op på taget. Derfra lød hendes klage: »Ve Gilgamesh! Han har dræbt Himmeltyren og fået mig til at græde!« Da Enkidu hørte Ishtars skrig, rev han et lem af tyren og kastede det hen foran hende: »Kunne jeg nå dig, gjorde jeg det samme ved dig! Jeg ville hænge dens tarme om dine hofter som et bælte.« Ishtar samlede de indviede kvinder, kurtisanerne og de prostituerede og sang klagesangen med dem over den legemsdel, der var revet af tyren.
When they had killed the Bull of Heaven they took out its heart and offered it to the sun god. Once they had poured their drink offering to the sun, the two brothers sat down. Ishtar climbed Uruk's high wall and went up on the roof. From there came her lament: “Woe to Gilgamesh! He has killed the Bull of Heaven and made me weep!” When Enkidu heard Ishtar screaming, he tore a limb from the bull and threw it down before her. “If I could reach you I'd do the same to you! I'd hang its guts around your hips like a belt.” Ishtar gathered the women dedicated to the gods, the courtesans and the prostitutes and led their lament over the limb torn from the bull.

Gilgamesh kaldte alle mestrene og kunsthåndværkerne til sig, så hvert håndværk kunne prise hornenes størrelse. På hvert horn sad en indfatning med tredive miner lapisblåt stenmateriale. To fingre […] Begge horn rummede seks mål olie. Olien viede han til sin gud Lugalbanda til salvelse. Hornene bar han ind og hængte op i sine forfædres helligdom.
Gilgamesh summoned all the master artisans and artists so every craft could praise the size of the horns. Each horn had a setting of thirty minas of lapis-blue stone. Two fingers […] The two horns held six measures of oil. He dedicated the oil to his god Lugalbanda for anointing and carried the horns in and hung them up in his ancestors' shrine.

De vaskede hænder i Eufrat og gik i optog ind i byen. De skred frem ad Uruks hovedgade, og Uruks krigere stimlede sammen for at se dem. Gilgamesh gav byens førende mænd en gåde: »Hvem er den prægtigste af heltene? Hvem er den navnkundigste af kæmperne? Gilgamesh er den prægtigste af heltene. Enkidu er den navnkundigste af kæmperne.« [Tre beskadigede linjer.] Så holdt Gilgamesh gilde i sit palads. Da alle krigerne lå og sov på deres lejer, faldt også Enkidu i søvn og fik et syn. Han kom til sin ven og fortalte sin drøm.
They washed their hands in the Euphrates and entered the city in procession. They strode down Uruk's main street, and Uruk's warriors crowded round to see them. Gilgamesh posed a riddle to the city's leading men. “Who is most splendid among heroes? Who is most renowned among giants? Gilgamesh is most splendid among heroes. Enkidu is most renowned among giants.” [Three damaged lines.] Then Gilgamesh held a feast in his palace. When all the warriors lay asleep on their beds, Enkidu too fell asleep and had a vision. He came to his friend and told his dream.
